## Statement

Write with quantifiers the statement "the real sequence $(u_n)$ converges to $\ell$". Then write its negation, and the negation of "$(u_n)$ converges".

## Hint

Swap each $\forall$ with $\exists$ (and the other way round) and negate the final inequality.

## Solution

**Convergence to $\ell$.**
$$\forall\varepsilon>0,\ \exists N\in\mathbb{N},\ \forall n\ge N,\ |u_n-\ell|<\varepsilon.$$

**Negation.**
$$\exists\varepsilon>0,\ \forall N\in\mathbb{N},\ \exists n\ge N,\ |u_n-\ell|\ge\varepsilon.$$
In words: from some fixed distance $\varepsilon$, infinitely many terms stay at least $\varepsilon$ away from $\ell$.

**Negation of "$(u_n)$ converges".** The sequence converges if there exists some $\ell$ with the property above. Its negation is
$$\forall\ell\in\mathbb{R},\ \exists\varepsilon>0,\ \forall N\in\mathbb{N},\ \exists n\ge N,\ |u_n-\ell|\ge\varepsilon.$$
